the subspace $\\text{Span}{v,w}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 15 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 34\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9fd0121-5ce9-5a7b-a706-59ba73a71f7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix That Defines an Orthogonal Projection Onto the Subspace Spanned by a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4214a5fa-1444-5a1c-9f3b-f23356d16a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395032} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7c54e1-2753-5b3b-8dbc-c092bf88a030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 26 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395033} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdf1c82-4a5f-5f56-802e-3bf2e7216f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395034} {:answer/id #uuid "19c0d11b-b024-5e01-ab9d-531f4d5f045f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395035} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9e05e-f1dc-59de-9514-061c591fda4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 26 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4214a5fa-1444-5a1c-9f3b-f23356d16a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395032} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8265b3-ec05-5f71-a9dc-0c0e3e740494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e06ca66-7dd3-5561-91f7-cf5648b2521e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95204" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}{v,w}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -16 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0741fad6-3e88-53a4-bb5d-f80e445f8778" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Projection Matrix Given Vectors That Span the Orthogonal Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71d40fd-d37f-57f8-a7e4-057e4bcb2fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395039} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d009d-6826-587b-8387-3ee605797a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395040} {:answer/id #uuid "790a3985-ddc6-524a-aed0-98ee33485d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395041} {:answer/id #uuid "5223971a-bdfb-5d61-b209-82a3449833f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395042} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b2ece8-bc5c-5bc4-92d6-6e0659f21498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5b2ece8-bc5c-5bc4-92d6-6e0659f21498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395043} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3bd339-81a5-59c7-8cfd-ae90fe4d1726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a78f43a-1483-5095-8c62-d59f02500754" :question/math-academy-id "q-95366" :question/problem "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}{a}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={18}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}17 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 17 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}=(-4)+4=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "794d4c57-637a-5193-940b-4ff7ac74a55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395046} {:answer/id #uuid "27d3309c-3a7e-5f1c-908b-6094dc352bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395047} {:answer/id #uuid "380b57b2-e791-5039-9d04-a9810497ecb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395048} {:answer/id #uuid "4f13293f-f4e6-5635-a0a7-6f82d5e89e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395049} {:answer/id #uuid "870b3347-21ff-559c-b1a9-0d01055f883c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "380b57b2-e791-5039-9d04-a9810497ecb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395048} :answer-field/id #uuid "a837d983-c570-594e-8c53-2aa0ddbba19d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e789a84c-c48c-5446-b3d4-f3e93d458a29" :question/math-academy-id "q-95704" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ad9ead-87b5-5eaf-8e9e-875db0473a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395053} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc2aec0-03c1-52f2-822c-b5b8cc4452d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} {:answer/id #uuid "a3017386-cdc1-5c67-aaa3-da1bf9ee139b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395055} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae42751-57d2-54a9-a3c9-6c4df9c9f145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395056} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f3f42-28e8-5a6e-8c4e-682350ff961b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3017386-cdc1-5c67-aaa3-da1bf9ee139b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395055} :answer-field/id #uuid "659dcf86-3da2-503b-9a49-309b10754236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7d41eb-9b50-57b5-bc80-ebc06a06e4f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95708" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations which is already in row echelon form and has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=1.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395060} {:answer/id #uuid "3b601ff3-863f-5502-84d6-13f00e2ad45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395061} {:answer/id #uuid "84da8f15-dc10-55f3-97d6-434ca1ff643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395062} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb077ea-553a-5aa6-a64a-7a2b84621b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5[\\begin{aligned}80 & 60 \\\\ 60 & 45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395063} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d006a-6b5c-519d-a0ac-32bc5de9d2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395060} :answer-field/id #uuid "3211a6bb-133a-5e40-90d8-fc361c03ff47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05f92170-0d3f-5d3a-aa67-7dea2e387ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95710" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d6bb6a-6b5d-5930-ae9a-9b839dcdddb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395067} {:answer/id #uuid "0347e36c-6386-5361-936c-dfdf6232dced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} {:answer/id #uuid "66380990-ae13-5c02-b2dc-9fd55b82a145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14 \\\\ 14 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395069} {:answer/id #uuid "5e73c68a-ab95-5792-acd8-5957407b5f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395070} {:answer/id #uuid "f3057db3-3c43-50e8-88bc-8548c42643cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e73c68a-ab95-5792-acd8-5957407b5f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395070} :answer-field/id #uuid "425b9a82-dba0-5520-8aa0-36f3f21fc4a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "180de339-1f27-54e7-82a8-44bcd599a7e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95718" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-12,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-75 & -300 \\\\ -300 & 100\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d17ddb9-9738-5247-980d-6346f34ca3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395074} {:answer/id #uuid "9112c274-741d-5926-a7e8-28550395e3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395075} {:answer/id #uuid "a6833e0f-d872-5f84-8fad-2cd587942a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395076} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a1eeb9-2422-526b-996e-3116b894cad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395077} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3216f5-0141-54bf-b967-4b7d4a78081b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d17ddb9-9738-5247-980d-6346f34ca3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395074} :answer-field/id #uuid "da07241e-4465-5487-a804-a1f93208770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f741f54-e9ba-55b4-9c81-df73745d51c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95724" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc49024-62c3-5852-bb10-f8ca661deb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395081} {:answer/id #uuid "189c99b8-b12d-5bce-8a73-8432708f26d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395082} {:answer/id #uuid "22327ee0-66ed-5f0b-ad74-16db425a88e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395083} {:answer/id #uuid "9c64ad08-76b8-5693-907f-147d2c3ef11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395084} {:answer/id #uuid "4535b0b1-6fc1-52dd-b0ee-ea5c7d2feecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "189c99b8-b12d-5bce-8a73-8432708f26d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395082} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a48dfc1-7406-5a03-80fb-7e45d0584999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37b1654d-7a67-5975-aaea-e4e904cbf870" :question/math-academy-id "q-95729" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-24,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}25 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 25\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 130 \\\\ 130 & -598\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8743bfaf-2769-54ae-a264-86b8504ad546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395087} {:answer/id #uuid "f51d771b-a16a-52b0-9a9c-0bdd9295baab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395088} {:answer/id #uuid "7da7d279-0192-57c9-a120-edf1b3f008c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395089} {:answer/id #uuid "795b09e9-e6c6-5744-8a17-7836bc583588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395090} {:answer/id #uuid "32f1cc2f-8169-599b-ba00-8d5ad043bc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51d771b-a16a-52b0-9a9c-0bdd9295baab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395088} :answer-field/id #uuid "b63c485f-1fdc-5f96-8275-f5fdc78f32ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5df0e730-65fe-51be-be3c-4af036828478" :question/math-academy-id "q-95731" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332ee583-e727-5bba-8acd-922d23695b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395094} {:answer/id #uuid "a10add95-adc6-5235-9f97-1b43eca22a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395095} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26ab61-b779-5a11-875e-496dff8002aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395096} {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395097} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4b6724-9b4a-518a-bc3a-5a3c01258664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395097} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a695-b25d-5276-8883-5cca1cebedbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5db18361-738b-5a9a-b3b0-1db51bcde1bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95911" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-100I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & -60\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}60 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584e164f-8516-5a89-8d2f-685827b1e801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395101} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0909e5-2262-548d-8922-838f4b6fd28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395102} {:answer/id #uuid "89951ce5-7b67-5119-8f30-c55ee6ac3019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395103} {:answer/id #uuid "94c8687a-b231-5c45-9224-42be9d7b68e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395104} {:answer/id #uuid "88c76ebd-3dd2-5f87-bbf7-9d841757f331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584e164f-8516-5a89-8d2f-685827b1e801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395101} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e1f306c-9388-51c9-984a-8d6a8e4dd681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5112a112-d520-578f-a615-69da3e2d4aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96019" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73f4b81-f7c2-58b3-ba59-81c1cb4e21d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395108} {:answer/id #uuid "2965e000-8ed9-5b43-8807-b337179e3e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395109} {:answer/id #uuid "f728b525-0316-5b5a-bee0-0f67b27c8544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395110} {:answer/id #uuid "5578869c-e22f-59c6-b9c4-7f6a4a040091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395111} {:answer/id #uuid "db6dd2c4-cdf3-588c-b826-6965e60d9428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5578869c-e22f-59c6-b9c4-7f6a4a040091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395111} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8d465ac-6125-5625-8e53-c378575651c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a11a1ea5-30ff-5154-92c7-cf0778166ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96026" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{2}{7} & \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8b3e92-0e0d-5d19-bb51-bfe62ab510a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395115} {:answer/id #uuid "e43f1009-aa53-5355-b6e2-6d08c550cf8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395116} {:answer/id #uuid "37c89cba-4da8-53a7-87c2-9de05914aaba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395117} {:answer/id #uuid "56b92eb0-336a-5fcf-8448-085005b2ce34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395118} {:answer/id #uuid "c551338c-b292-5e26-8b55-ecd82904ce69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c551338c-b292-5e26-8b55-ecd82904ce69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395119} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca9ce0b-73b4-529a-afb7-e8bec3879243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ae0f219-c74d-5218-aeb5-b468ecc59fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-96036" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-3)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12756fea-d511-5413-ba42-e73f9ba0c031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: Q → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171400} {:answer/id #uuid "2a18c8bc-4ef6-50fd-a7fb-41e818cc22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: X → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171401} {:answer/id #uuid "3809dc43-4135-5464-91b6-25b44f1ab772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171402} {:answer/id #uuid "43760b88-aeaa-5fdb-b186-992ef13e2b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: X → Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171403} {:answer/id #uuid "59cf8c2b-0ce0-5a3c-a0a7-b3fdd9e769b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171404}] :answer-field/id #uuid "920e367e-2ddb-534f-b346-fd7c372957ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171399}] :question/id #uuid "25726fd8-78e9-529e-acdf-5c4c5a237b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-96040" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n> *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers.\"*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec0e316-618a-5c06-8ef7-151ecbbb1a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfd9789a91f680d5105d9081e51103ffba5e986fa4a96331cde06df6d0c42854.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395122} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a52671-312b-5bd4-be3f-4aa0a30f8a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/543365359599203eb6f9e8824f6e3b2987a1fec9121568dd7a9098bfc16870f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395123} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1ba541-6bb3-5c12-a669-2035625bf666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3edf063230f100455638fa25f28a4c5e59e2e54884d9e273724b76ae483aa0bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395124} {:answer/id #uuid "956373bb-29e5-548d-9797-5b1c1b94804e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/631f9d7772354c6962f98e857371eac65d0fe36116bbae60dacd0db7ba01bc04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395125} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6386d1-d302-5559-a668-c92b3aa1ae83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/7670c9662fec950b45f2f939ed416590d278875b168caea9d7e5b70f7c8c6a9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a52671-312b-5bd4-be3f-4aa0a30f8a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/543365359599203eb6f9e8824f6e3b2987a1fec9121568dd7a9098bfc16870f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395123} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d543f0-8732-53ef-a971-f59ec7730788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53548ea4-2b92-5da8-9bc4-e7dc116ebef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96089" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=-2+2(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1+2(1)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2+2(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cc/cc55a08f71cd75f0dda356149a61e9ab78536ecbb4644b88f840122863b92b88.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd82220-ad9a-5d23-9059-b9e91a7e8ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395129} {:answer/id #uuid "c425653f-089a-5c30-8706-e4cbc10d613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395130} {:answer/id #uuid "9c91095b-22b2-54a7-b096-79affb1e80fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395131} {:answer/id #uuid "9e80b2f7-83bf-5266-afbf-542740fe0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395132} {:answer/id #uuid "aeddcf0b-022c-51c6-96fa-c8ec1b75756b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e80b2f7-83bf-5266-afbf-542740fe0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395132} :answer-field/id #uuid "af024373-62e6-5fa0-a940-575a299d6f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df080fa-5765-5104-a801-ac51915d6839" :question/math-academy-id "q-96318" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{40}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca91e9e-e238-5213-ab51-7b27677efb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166011} {:answer/id #uuid "a568f08a-e8f9-50b4-bda6-969ce6cedea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166012} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d9910f-59fb-51a5-98b9-e14efb05ea1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $V = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166013} {:answer/id #uuid "f978b995-377e-5670-9d14-29acb3107c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166014} {:answer/id #uuid "28b56754-724b-57af-a4bb-44fd395efa42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166015}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a6091cef-0dc2-5c6e-8ecd-d1cf1c9133cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166010}] :question/id #uuid "b0a7a3b6-717f-5db5-9c33-ba5621dfa097" :question/math-academy-id "q-96351" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754fb709-47af-5bd6-88d7-3ea678b575f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e7bed1d871dee02cd97ff7b0ed03f3a2897eee67e77b3a37444959fe77cb332.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395135} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaac902-7231-5a55-ba31-2680f02cbad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7acc3f95c6cd63875373f724b003f1f66126e31f53d4eadf5b64ae847d44b664.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395136} {:answer/id #uuid "8c888613-a032-5656-8f71-4d52aebe0531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89f14c12dcba118efe08cec5cf0248d345b9a190d2d8501629064323b083f013.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395137} {:answer/id #uuid "ce936766-5e6c-5a45-a4ab-78c7c29d170c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4110a4703541182a990cfa7cbe6061cc03d0b70749212ea9bcf550c046f43da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395138} {:answer/id #uuid "b32ffe61-68d6-56a1-a60a-ae51d002b1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b32ffe61-68d6-56a1-a60a-ae51d002b1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395139} :answer-field/id #uuid "f151b7cc-ca7f-5fb1-ade0-f4d11b7b06bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79dc5bea-a8e5-5bec-830b-c8eb6fa1519c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96515" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbccf40aca753c4c23cc2b159697deca70b45844d2cbf458c9a04f6dc6d3026f.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y<-2.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in this region.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $-2<y<0$ or $y>0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in these regions.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](images/64/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png)"}]}
